//! MEMORY — a range of physical memory, and the one op it owns (spec §2.5;
//! sawos design 2 D-2, grown by design 6 D-2).
//!
//! It sits one step above the floor and names nothing else, which is what M3
//! unit 4 had to keep true while giving the object ops. `split` is here because
//! it needs only a Memory; `map` is NOT, because it needs a `Process` and
//! `Process` is declared in `sos.system`, which imports this file. See
//! `Process.map` there for the shape that cycle forced and why the funnel reads
//! the way it does.
//!
//! A region is still AUTHORITY OVER BYTES rather than a description of them:
//! there is no way to read a Memory's base or length, and unit 4 did not add
//! one. `split` names a LENGTH and `map` names a target, and both answer with
//! capabilities rather than numbers.

import sosabi.{SosStatus, MemoryHandle, MemoryOp, MINT_OP, NO_HANDLE}

import sos.floor.{ALL_RIGHTS, call_value, sos_handle_release}

/// A handle to a Memory — a sealed range of physical memory.
///
/// §2.5's RAM AUTHORITY. A process receives pool roots at boot through
/// `Process.boot_handle_next`, DERIVES pieces of them with `split`, INSTALLS
/// those pieces with `Process.map`, and passes whole regions to
/// `System.process_create`.
///
/// **THERE IS STILL NO WAY TO READ THE BOUNDS**, which is the unit-2 property
/// unit 4 deliberately kept: a region is authority over bytes, not a
/// description of them. What a caller names is a LENGTH (`split`) or a TARGET
/// (`map`), and what it gets back is another capability.
///
/// Built by `Process.boot_handle_next` and by `split`, and by nothing else — no
/// word constructor, no word extractor, the module docstring's rule.
///
/// **IT IS `NoCopy`, AND DROPPING IT RELEASES THE HANDLE** (sawos design 3 D-5),
/// which SUPERSEDES this docstring's own unit-2 paragraph explaining why it was
/// a plain value. Both reasons that paragraph gave have been answered rather
/// than overridden. The OWNING tier is here, so a move-only handle's drop now
/// does something — it is the enforcement the discipline was waiting for. And
/// the boot-handle record's shape is what changed to fit: see `BootHandle` in
/// `sos.system`, whose `Memory` is an optional the caller TAKES rather than a
/// field it would have had to partially move out of.
public struct Memory {
    public(package) handle: MemoryHandle,
}

extension Memory: NoCopy {
    /// Drop is release; see `System`'s deinit for the sentinel and the discard.
    func deinit(&var self) {
        if self.handle != NO_HANDLE {
            let _ = sos_handle_release(self.handle)
        }
    }
}

extension Memory {
    /// **CUT `len` BYTES OFF THE FRONT, and become the remainder.** Requires
    /// `MemoryRight.Split`.
    ///
    /// **THIS RECEIVER IS THE POOL CURSOR**, which is the whole of design 6
    /// D-2's allocation story: the region that comes back covers `[base, base +
    /// len)` and THIS ONE now covers everything after it, so repeated splits
    /// walk a pool forward and nothing else has to be remembered. Allocation is
    /// this method in a loop.
    ///
    /// **IT CONSUMES NOTHING.** `&self`, no `move`, no disarm — the parent
    /// handle is the same handle afterwards and the caller keeps using it. What
    /// changed is kernel-side (the region the handle names is now shorter),
    /// which is exactly the asymmetry that makes this NOT a transfer funnel: no
    /// word moved between tables, so the owning tier has nothing to say here.
    ///
    /// `len` must be a non-zero multiple of the target's protection
    /// granularity, and no larger than what this region still holds. All three
    /// are caller-checkable, so all three TERMINATE the caller — the caller
    /// chose the number, and it knows what it has split off so far. A full slab
    /// or handle table is `SosStatus.NoResource`, which it could not have known.
    ///
    /// **DROPPING THE PIECE GIVES BACK THE SLOT AND THE BYTES** (sawos design
    /// 28, on design 25 D-5; M3 unit 5's design 7 D-1 landed the first half).
    /// The release that takes a region's last reference to zero returns its
    /// kernel SLAB SLOT and credits the caller's Memory quota row — and returns
    /// its RANGE to the pool it was ultimately split from, where it coalesces
    /// with its free neighbours and, if it touches the pool's unspent frontier,
    /// folds back into it. A pool walked to its end and then released is a pool
    /// again: `tests/memory-recycle` takes one to its last byte, gives the
    /// pieces back out of order, and splits the whole thing out in one call.
    ///
    /// **THE LAST REFERENCE IS NOT ALWAYS THE LAST HANDLE**, which is the one
    /// thing to know. §2.5 frees a region when the last reference of EITHER kind
    /// drops, and an installed mapping is the other kind — so a region that is
    /// still MAPPED somewhere keeps its bytes after every handle onto it is
    /// gone, and they come home at the last `unmap` (or at the death of the
    /// process whose domain holds the row). Dropping a region you have mapped
    /// into a child is therefore safe and eventually tidy, rather than safe and
    /// permanent as it was through M4.
    ///
    /// This note used to say the opposite in as many words — that a front-cut
    /// parent cannot absorb an arbitrary hole, so the bytes belong to no pool
    /// afterwards and a pool walked to its end stays walked. That was the honest
    /// state of the system until this unit and is what the unit was for.
    public func split(&self, len: UInt) -> Result<Memory, SosStatus> {
        let raw = try call_value(self.handle, MemoryOp.Split as UInt, len, 0, 0)
        return Memory(handle: MemoryHandle(raw))
    }

    /// **MINT A SIBLING of this region handle, narrowed by a KEEP MASK** — the
    /// universal `MINT_OP`, on `System.mint`'s exact terms. Requires the
    /// universal `MemoryRight.Mint` on THIS handle.
    ///
    /// **THIS IS WHERE A LAUNCHER'S MEMORY POLICY IS WRITTEN** (sawos design 9
    /// D-1). A region's default set is permissive — `memory_rights()` carries
    /// `Transfer | Mint | Split | Map | MapExecute`, and it has to, because
    /// attenuation is monotonic and a bit not minted at boot could never appear
    /// later — so what a child may do with the RAM it is handed is decided HERE,
    /// once, by the mask the launcher writes:
    ///
    /// ```saw
    /// let for_child = pool_page.mint(rights: MemoryRight.Transfer
    ///                                        | MemoryRight.Map)!
    /// child.give(memory: move for_child, tag: RAM)
    /// ```
    ///
    /// That child can install its page and read and write it, and can never
    /// execute from it — not because the kernel asks who is calling, but because
    /// nothing it holds carries `MapExecute`. `Split` is absent from that mask
    /// too, which is the honest way to say "here is your RAM, you may not
    /// subdivide it for anyone".
    ///
    /// **THE MASK MUST KEEP `Transfer` if the sibling is to be GIVEN**, exactly
    /// as `Process.give(system:)` records: `give` checks that bit on the thing
    /// being given, so a launcher cannot narrow below the ticket that lets the
    /// narrowed handle arrive.
    ///
    /// Everything else `System.mint` says holds unchanged: the rights are this
    /// handle's INTERSECTED with the mask (so no-amplification is structural and
    /// naming an absent right is a no-op), `Mint` is itself maskable, the
    /// sibling is INDEPENDENT — its own slot, its own generation, its own
    /// release — and it is a second NAME onto one region rather than a second
    /// region. A full handle table is `SosStatus.NoResource`; a handle without
    /// `Mint` TERMINATES the caller.
    public func mint(&self,
                     rights: UInt32 = ALL_RIGHTS) -> Result<Memory, SosStatus> {
        let raw = try call_value(self.handle, MINT_OP, rights as UInt, 0, 0)
        return Memory(handle: MemoryHandle(raw))
    }
}
